# Intellectual Property and Invention Disclosure Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — 501(c)(3) Nonprofit Research Institute
EIN: {{ ein }} | Incorporated in {{ state }}

## 1. Purpose

This policy governs the ownership, disclosure, protection, and disposition of intellectual property created in connection with the research and programmatic activities of {{ org_name }} ("the Organization"). It ensures compliance with the Bayh-Dole Act (35 USC 200-212), implementing regulations (37 CFR 401), and Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.315), while supporting the Organization's mission to advance scientific knowledge and public benefit.

## 2. Scope

This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, visiting researchers, and collaborators ("Covered Individuals") who:

1. Use {{ org_name }} resources (equipment, facilities, data, funding, or personnel time) in the course of their work;
2. Perform work under any grant, contract, or cooperative agreement administered by the Organization; or
3. Participate in research projects directed or managed by the Organization.

Covered Individuals must acknowledge this policy in writing as a condition of engagement.

## 3. Ownership of Inventions

### 3.1 General Rule

Inventions conceived or first actually reduced to practice using {{ org_name }} resources, or within the scope of a Covered Individual's duties to the Organization, are the property of {{ org_name }}. Covered Individuals shall assign all right, title, and interest in such inventions to the Organization upon request.

### 3.2 Federal Funding — Bayh-Dole Rights

For inventions arising under federally funded research, ownership is subject to the rights of the United States government as prescribed by the Bayh-Dole Act and the terms of the applicable funding agreement. The Organization elects to retain title to such inventions in accordance with the procedures in Section 4.

### 3.3 Excluded Inventions

This policy does not claim ownership of inventions that are:

- Conceived and reduced to practice entirely on the inventor's own time;
- Made without use of any {{ org_name }} resources; and
- Unrelated to the Organization's research programs or the inventor's duties.

An inventor who believes an invention falls within this exclusion should still disclose it under Section 5 so that the Organization can confirm the exclusion in writing.

## 4. Bayh-Dole Compliance (Federally Funded Inventions)

When an invention arises under a federal funding agreement, the Organization shall comply with the following obligations. The Executive Director (or designee) is responsible for ensuring each deadline is met.

### 4.1 Disclosure to the Federal Agency

The Organization shall report the invention to the sponsoring federal agency in writing within **two (2) months** of receiving the inventor's disclosure under Section 5.

### 4.2 Election of Title

The Organization shall elect in writing whether to retain title to the invention within **two (2) years** of disclosing the invention to the federal agency (or such shorter period as the funding agreement requires).

### 4.3 Patent Filing

If the Organization elects to retain title, it shall file a patent application within **one (1) year** of election, or before any statutory bar date, whichever is earlier. The Organization shall notify the federal agency of each filing and maintain the patent through its term.

### 4.4 Government License

The federal government retains a nonexclusive, nontransferable, irrevocable, paid-up license to practice the invention, or have it practiced on its behalf, throughout the world. This license is automatic and does not require separate negotiation.

### 4.5 U.S. Manufacturing Preference

Any products embodying or produced through the use of a federally funded invention shall be substantially manufactured in the United States, unless the sponsoring agency grants a waiver under 35 USC 204.

### 4.6 March-In Rights

The sponsoring federal agency may exercise march-in rights under 35 USC 203 if the Organization or its licensees fail to take effective steps to achieve practical application of the invention within a reasonable time, or if action is necessary to meet health, safety, or other public-use requirements.

## 5. Inventor Disclosure Procedure

### 5.1 Obligation to Disclose

Every Covered Individual must disclose to the Executive Director any potentially patentable invention within **thirty (30) days** of conception or first actual reduction to practice, whichever occurs first.

### 5.2 Disclosure Form

Disclosures shall be submitted in writing (email or the Organization's invention disclosure form) and must include:

1. A description of the invention in sufficient detail to evaluate patentability;
2. The names of all inventors and their institutional affiliations;
3. The funding source(s) (grant or contract numbers) that supported the work;
4. The date of conception and, if applicable, the date of first reduction to practice;
5. Any public disclosures already made (publications, presentations, poster sessions) and their dates; and
6. Any known prior art.

### 5.3 Evaluation

The Executive Director shall evaluate each disclosure, consulting patent counsel as appropriate, and shall notify the inventor(s) within sixty (60) days whether the Organization will:

- Pursue patent protection;
- Release the invention to the inventor(s); or
- Dedicate the invention to the public domain.

## 6. Revenue Sharing

If the Organization commercializes an invention through licensing, sale, or other disposition, a share of net licensing revenue (gross revenue less direct costs of patent prosecution, maintenance, and licensing) shall be distributed to the inventor(s). The specific percentage shall be established by the Board of Directors prior to the first distribution event and documented as an addendum to this policy.

Revenue sharing does not apply to income from grants, contracts, or sponsored research agreements — only to commercialization proceeds.

## 7. Copyrights

### 7.1 Works Made for Hire

Works of authorship created by employees within the scope of their employment, or by contractors under agreements designating the work as "work made for hire," are the property of {{ org_name }}.

### 7.2 Federally Funded Works

For works produced under federal funding, the Organization retains copyright subject to the government's right to reproduce, publish, or otherwise use the work, and to authorize others to do so, for government purposes (2 CFR 200.315(b)). The Organization shall comply with any open-access requirements imposed by the funding agency (e.g., NIH Public Access Policy, OSTP Nelson Memo).

### 7.3 Software

Software created under {{ org_name }}-directed research is subject to the same ownership rules as other copyrightable works. See Section 8 for open-source considerations.

## 8. Open Source and Publication

### 8.1 Default to Open

Consistent with its nonprofit research mission, the Organization defaults to open publication of fundamental research results, including datasets, software, and methodologies. Proprietary restrictions are applied only when:

- Required by the terms of a funding agreement or collaboration;
- Necessary to protect patentable inventions during the patent-filing window; or
- Justified by a written determination from the Executive Director that commercialization serves the Organization's mission.

### 8.2 Pre-Publication Review

When research is funded under an agreement that grants the sponsor a right of pre-publication review, the inventor or author must submit the manuscript to the Executive Director at least thirty (30) days before the intended submission date so that the Organization can meet its contractual obligations and evaluate the need for patent filings.

## 9. STTR and SBIR Intellectual Property

When {{ org_name }} participates as the Research Institution (RI) partner in a Small Business Technology Transfer (STTR) or Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) award, IP allocation shall follow the terms of the negotiated IP agreement between the Organization and the small business partner, as required by 15 USC 638. The Executive Director shall ensure that each STTR/SBIR agreement includes a written IP allocation clause before work begins.

## 10. Record Retention

Invention disclosures, patent prosecution files, licensing agreements, assignment documents, and related correspondence shall be retained for the **life of the patent plus three (3) years**. For inventions where no patent is filed, records shall be retained for ten (10) years from the date of disclosure. This schedule is supplementary to the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## 11. Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually to ensure it reflects current federal requirements, organizational practices, and mission priorities.

## 12. Adoption

This policy becomes effective on merge to `main`; the merge commit and MR approval record are the official adoption record.

